---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');

// 這頁刻意是「有日期的」：/lab/ 講方法、/platform/ 講規模，這裡講一次
// 完整的搬遷是怎麼排序的。Day 1 = 2026-07-20，Day 12 = 2026-07-31，每一列
// 都對得上 nplus-infra / nplus-gitops 的 commit，所以不要改成約略說法。
const days = [
  {
    date: 'Day 1',
    what: 'A contract, a preview and an automatic rollback',
    why: 'Before moving anything: the deploy learned to print what it would change, to check every configuration value before applying it, and to return to the last good version on a failed verification. Then the rollback was tested by deliberately breaking production twice.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 1',
    what: 'Kubernetes arrives, and three services move in',
    why: 'Cluster, GitOps controller, app-of-apps, and a disposable demo workload deleted from the declaration to prove that removal propagates. Then three services entered the cluster while the old copies kept running — traffic moved by editing one upstream.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 1',
    what: 'Monitoring and databases start their own dual runs',
    why: 'The metrics stack entered as an observer of the old one rather than a replacement, and the first database moved to an operator-managed cluster. Two more parallel worlds to keep in sync — which is the price of never having a moment where only the new thing exists.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 2',
    what: 'Secrets, certificates and the firewall become declarations',
    why: 'Encrypted secrets in version control, a wildcard certificate issued over DNS, the firewall stated as a file with a script that verifies the running machine against it. Rebuilding this box stopped being an act of memory.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 2',
    what: 'A rollback ate somebody’s work',
    why: 'An automatic rollback reset a working tree that had uncommitted writing in it. The fix was a rule — never roll back a dirty tree — and a note recording what was lost. The incident is in the repository because a mechanism that has never hurt you has not been tested.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 3',
    what: 'Everything gets pinned, encrypted and serialised',
    why: 'Images pinned by digest, every backup encrypted, and concurrent deploys serialised — the last one after a race had been misdiagnosed as a network flake for most of a day.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 5',
    what: 'The restore drill runs on the new engine',
    why: 'A backup that has not been restored is a belief. The drill was re-pointed at the new database version and run until it passed.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 7',
    what: 'The cutovers, and the retirements that make them real',
    why: 'The edge, the certificates and the monitoring authority all moved to the cluster — and the old proxy, the old certificate renewer, the old metrics stack and the old dashboards were deleted the same day. A cold-boot drill passed, and the watchdog got a heartbeat that lives off the machine.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 11',
    what: 'A service is retired for lack of traffic',
    why: 'Seven days of no real requests was the criterion, decided in advance; the data was left untouched. Log aggregation landed the same week, so the cluster stopped being the only part of the system with no memory.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 12',
    what: 'The database engine changes underneath the application',
    why: 'The last application moved off the legacy engine onto the operator-managed cluster, with the schema generated from the code rather than hand-written, and three engine differences recorded as they were hit.',
  },
  {
    date: 'Day 12',
    what: 'The pipeline instruments itself',
    why: 'Immutable image tags, because a mutable tag made the "Synced" badge a lie; the last application secrets moved out of CI; and the deployment system finally got alerts of its own. It had been the least monitored component on the machine.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Twelve days in July — Andrew Yang"
  description="A dated log of one migration: a hand-run machine became a declared one in twelve days, with every cutover dual-run first and every replaced component deleted the same day."
>
  <span class="tag">migration · dated</span>
  <h1>Twelve days in July</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The <a href={`${base}/lab/`}>lab</a> pages argue for a method. This page is
    the receipt: the twelve days in which a machine I ran by hand became one
    that is described by files, told in the order it actually happened. Nothing
    here is a plan — every line landed, and every line has a commit behind it.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 190"
    alt="Three states left to right. A hand-run machine, where the truth is what is running. Then a dual-run period where the old and new systems are both alive and traffic is moved between them. Then a declared machine, where the truth is a file and the running system is a consequence. Below the middle state, a dashed box shows the retirements that end each dual run: the replaced component is deleted, not left running."
    caption="The middle box is the whole discipline. Every step spent time in it, and none of them was finished until the box below had been emptied."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={30} h={64} title="Hand-run" sub="the truth is whatever" sub2="is currently running" />
    <DBox x={335} y={30} h={64} accent title="Dual-run" sub="both alive, traffic moved" sub2="one upstream at a time" />
    <DBox x={660} y={30} h={64} title="Declared" sub="the truth is a file; the running" sub2="system is a consequence" />
    <DBox x={320} y={120} w={260} h={60} dashed title="Retire the old one" sub="the same day, or it never happened" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,62 L329,62" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M565,62 L654,62" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M450,94 L450,114" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
  </Diagram>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Day</th>
          <th>What landed</th>
          <th>Why it is on this list</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          days.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{d.date}</td>
              <td>{d.what}</td>
              <td>{d.why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The four rules it was run by</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing is cut over before it has been dual-run.</strong> The new
      thing enters as an observer or a second copy, traffic moves in one
      reversible edit, and only then does the old one go. It costs a period of
      keeping two worlds in sync; it buys never having a moment where the only
      copy is the untested one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The retirement is part of the change, not the follow-up.</strong>
      A cutover that leaves the old component running has not reduced anything —
      it has added a second thing to maintain and a second story about what is
      true.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rollback comes from the last known-good deploy, not from
      history.</strong> Reasoning backwards through commits assumes the last
      commit is what is running, which is exactly the assumption a rollback
      invalidates. The pipeline records what it deployed and returns to that.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A drill counts when it has been run — twice.</strong> Cold boot,
      restore, rollback: each one was executed against the real machine, and the
      second run is what proves the first was not luck.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I would do differently</h2>
  <p>
    Four phases in one day was too fast — not because anything broke that could
    not be recovered, but because the rollback path got its first real exercise
    by accident rather than by design, and because a mechanism written that
    morning deleted work that afternoon. The order was right; the spacing was
    not. The parts I would repeat unchanged are the boring ones: verify the
    configuration before applying it, delete the thing you replaced, and write
    down the incident even when the only witness is you.
  </p>

  <p>
    The method behind this is spread across
    <a href={`${base}/lab/one-machine-one-operator/`}>one machine, one
    operator</a>, <a href={`${base}/lab/subtraction-as-a-feature/`}>subtraction
    as a feature</a>, and
    <a href={`${base}/lab/earned-green/`}>earned green</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
